## Context

Child of 0700. Read-only inspection on 2026-10-09 of `slim()` in `scripts/catalog_rel_sud_search.py`, used by the t1530, t1652 and t1790 OpenAlex lanes.

The request already selects `primary_location` and `authorships` (`OA_SELECT`), so the data arrives and `slim()` discards it. It keeps: id, DOI, title, year, date, language, type, cited_by_count, `journal` (source display name), affiliation countries, the abstract cut to 1,500 characters. It drops author names (the delivery writes `first_author` and `all_authors` empty in every row of these lanes), the host organization (publisher), source type, ISSN and landing page. The stored `results.jsonl.gz` holds only the slim records, so already-harvested works need a re-fetch.

Counts on the lane deliveries (record-level, not distinct works; lanes overlap, and t1652 is two runs): authors empty on 100% of the t1530, t1650, t1652 and t1790-rerun rows; OpenAlex records without a journal or ISSN: t1530 3,949 and t1652 4,502 per run (book chapters, conference papers, dissertations, reports, preprints, books; e.g. Routledge 10.4324, World Bank 10.1596 and IDB 10.18235 items). Not yet measured on the real pool.

The 1654 chaining collector (`_rel_chaining.py`, branch `t1654-citation-chaining`, unmerged) already keeps `authorships`; this ticket brings the older lanes level with it.

`enrich_rel_venues_openalex.py` already fetches by OpenAlex id (batches up to 200) the source id and name, source type, ISSN-L, host organization id, name and lineage, DOAJ flag and landing URL into the venue table (`rel_work_venues.csv`). A work whose primary location has no source gets no host organization from OpenAlex.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `slim()` keeps author names (first and all), host organization name and id, source type, ISSN and ISSN-L, landing page, and the full abstract; the three lanes use one shared definition rather than copies.
- [ ] Positive control: a known work with authors and a Routledge/World Bank chapter without a source are checked first, and the sources of each returned field are quoted.
- [ ] For works already harvested, the missing authors and host organizations are backfilled by id-batched re-fetch (`filter=ids.openalex:...`, up to 200 ids per call, `select=id,authorships,primary_location,locations`). The author stated on 2026-10-09 that OpenAlex budget is available. The per-call cost is measured from `x-ratelimit-cost-usd` on one small probe batch first; total cost = measured cost x batches, labelled derived, with the key's validity read from the first response headers (the project key is `OPENALEX_API_KEY` in the keystore; without it the code silently falls back to the unauthenticated pool).
- [ ] The backfill reuses the metering of the search collectors: lane cap, daily floor on `x-ratelimit-remaining-usd`, stop on 429 or `insufficient` budget, prepaid balance read from `x-ratelimit-prepaid-remaining-usd`. It is resumable across days (the daily budget is 1 USD, about 10,000 credits) and records which ids are done, so an interrupted run is never counted complete.
- [ ] Coverage after backfill is reported per lane, period and language: share of works with authors, with a host organization, and the residue with neither (to be handled by the DOI-prefix fallback of 2043).
- [ ] Backfilled fields are appended, never overwriting delivered values; `qa_rel_intake.py` passes on a regenerated sample.
- [ ] Diff at most 14 content-bearing paths; scoped gate quoted (`selected:` and `skipped:`).

## Notes

The pool is larger than the lane deliveries: 389,291 works on 2026-10-01 plus 1654 round 1 (208,181 incremental works; the sum, about 597k, is derived and assumes no overlap). Coverage numbers must be recomputed on the real pool table after `make rel-pool-data`, not on the lane CSVs.
